<Article
  title="Independence — The Standing Commitment"
  heading="Independence"
  kicker="The standing commitment"
  description="No compensation from any party but the client. No interest in any contractor. The owner contracts directly with the builder. This page is the commitment, in full."
>
  <p>
    Independence is the product. Everything else we do — the reviews, the verification, the reporting — is
    only worth paying for because of who we answer to and who we don’t. So the commitment is written down,
    published, and standing. It applies to every engagement, without exception, and you may hold us to it in
    the engagement agreement itself.
  </p>

  <div class="not-prose">
    <CalloutBand kicker="The commitment">
      We are compensated by our client and by no one else. We hold no interest in any contractor, supplier,
      or manufacturer. We take and pay no referral fees. The owner signs the construction contract directly —
      never through us.
    </CalloutBand>
  </div>

  <h2>What each line means in practice</h2>
  <h3>One paymaster</h3>
  <p>
    Every dollar this firm earns is invoiced to the client. We take nothing from the other side of the table:
    no marketing allowances from manufacturers, no volume rebates from suppliers, no finder’s fees from
    builders grateful for an introduction. When we recommend a contractor, a consultant, or a product, the
    recommendation carries no revenue for us in any direction — which is the only condition under which a
    recommendation is worth anything.
  </p>
  <h3>No interest in any contractor</h3>
  <p>
    Neither the firm nor its principals hold ownership in any construction, supply, or manufacturing business.
    If that ever changes in a way that could touch a client’s project, the client hears it from us first, in
    writing, with the option to end the engagement.
  </p>
  <h3>You hold the contract</h3>
  <p>
    The construction contract is signed between you and your builder. We do not perform construction work, we
    do not contract for it, and we do not hire trades. Our authority is to advise, review, verify, coordinate,
    and report — yours is to decide. This is not only the honest structure; it is the one that keeps your
    representative genuinely outside the transaction they are watching.
  </p>
  <h3>The specialists we retain answer to you</h3>
  <p>
    Where an engagement needs independent specialists — structural review, cost consulting, site
    verification — we retain them on the client’s behalf, and they are paid through the engagement, not by
    the contractor. No specialist we bring to your project has a relationship with your builder.
  </p>

  <h2>About our industry background</h2>
  <p>
    A fair question, and we would rather raise it than wait for it: our principals come out of the
    construction industry, including senior roles on the builder’s side of the table. Is that a conflict?
  </p>
  <p>
    We think it is the qualification. The person best placed to tell you where the room is in a construction
    agreement is someone who used to draft them. What would make it a conflict is a continuing financial
    relationship with any builder — and that is precisely what the commitment above forbids. Past experience
    on the other side of the table, plus zero present interest in it, is what independent representation is
    supposed to look like. The alternative — a representative who has never seen how a contractor prices,
    schedules, and negotiates — is independent mainly in the sense of being uninformed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Who we are, in full, is on <a href="/who-we-are">the team page</a>, including where each of us has
    worked.
  </p>

  <h2>Confidentiality</h2>
  <p>
    Discretion is the default, not a request you have to make. We do not publish client names, project
    locations, photographs, or identifying details — ever — without written permission. Enquiries and
    engagement materials are held in confidence, and we are glad to execute your form of NDA before a first
    conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>If we ever fall short</h2>
  <p>
    Any client who believes we have breached this commitment may raise it directly with either principal and
    is entitled to a written response. The engagement agreement gives the client the right to terminate on a
    breach of independence. A firm built on one promise should be easy to fire for breaking it.
  </p>
</Article>
